# =============================================================================
#  .gitignore
# =============================================================================
#  NOTE ON SYNTAX, learned the hard way at v3.14.153: git does NOT support a
#  trailing comment on a pattern line. `source/models/  # weights` is the literal
#  pattern "source/models/  # weights" and matches nothing. Every commented line
#  in this file was therefore inert, including the two that keep 601 MB of
#  CC-BY-NC model weights and 103 MB of developer history out of the repository.
#  Comments go on their OWN line. Verified by `git check-ignore`, not by reading.
# =============================================================================

# ── Large / generated ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# dev/ SHIPS. The plans, the bug list, the edit log and all 68 guards are what
# make this contributable; excluding the whole directory leaves a cloner with the
# application and nothing else. Two parts of it do not ship.
dev/archive/

# repair_prov_dates.py keeps the pre-repair file beside the repaired one. The
# repaired file is what ships; the backup is a local safety net, and the real
# record of the change is the pre-edit copy in dev/archive/ plus the receipt in
# .prov_date_repair.json. Shipping both would put two versions of the same
# fixture in the repo, one of them known-wrong.
*.b105-bak

dev/_to_delete/

# Stale pandoc renders of dev/BUGS.md and dev/DEV_PLAN.md, made once on
# 2026-08-27. Nothing generates them, no edit-log entry records them, no guard
# reads them, and BUGS.html's newest version reference is v3.14.99 — so they are
# 1.3 MB of out-of-date duplicates of the two documents this project calls its
# single source of truth. Scoped to `dev/`, deliberately: `source/LingCoT.html`
# IS the application, and a bare `*.html` would exclude it.
dev/*.html

# NLLB weights, ~601 MB, CC-BY-NC 4.0 and so incompatible with an MIT repository.
# LICENSE.txt states they are not distributed; this is what makes that true.
source/models/

# ── Python environment ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo

# ── Runtime / generated ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Machine-specific NLLB tuning, written by corpus_optimize.py.
annotator_config.json
# Holds only the generated config above, so nothing here is ever tracked.
source/config/
# Annotation resume files, written by corpus_annotate.py.
*.cursor

# ── Fieldwork: never commit ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A participants file holds real people's names, affiliations, birth decades and
# contact details, usually under a consent agreement that did not anticipate
# worldwide publication. A corpus holds their recorded speech. Committed once,
# either is in the history and in every fork and clone already taken.
#
# This is a DEFAULT, not a guarantee: `git add -f` overrides it, and a file that
# is already tracked ignores it entirely. hooks/pre-commit is the check that
# refuses; see the install line at the top of that file. Both, because each one
# alone has a way past it.
*_participants.jsonl
*_corpus.jsonl
*_dictionary.jsonl
# Except the cleared, synthetic fixtures the guard suite runs against.
#
# B-156, v3.14.312: this negation is BROADER THAN ITS PURPOSE. It was written to
# re-admit the three fixture files above, and because it names a whole subtree it
# also re-admits anything else that lands there — including `*.b105-bak` (line
# 23), which is ignored everywhere in the repository except under `samples/`,
# where a later pattern won. Three backup files, 72 KB, would have gone into the
# first commit: two versions of the same fixture, one of them the known-wrong one
# line 23 exists to keep out.
#
# The same shape as the v3.14.153 defect this file's header commemorates — a
# pattern that reads as active and is inert — except that here it is inert only
# for a subset of paths, which is why reading the file did not show it. Found by
# `dev/tools/ship_disclosure.py` on its first run, before `git init` rather than
# after, which is the whole argument for running it.
#
# Re-stated after the negation rather than by narrowing it: `!samples/**` is
# doing a job, and a cleverer glob that did both jobs would be one nobody could
# read. `gitignore_test.js` holds BOTH directions, so neither this line nor the
# negation above can be widened without a failure.
!samples/**
samples/**/*.b105-bak

# And the purpose-built specimens under dev/tests/fixtures/, for the same reason
# and by the same mechanism.
#
# B-205, v3.14.389: `cli_ingested/` is `corpus_ingest.py`'s own output, built at
# v3.14.386 so `prov_intern_test`'s "not yet interned, must shrink" branch has a
# fixture again. It is named `cli-ingested_corpus.jsonl`, because that is the
# name the CLI writes and the loader resolves — so `*_corpus.jsonl` above caught
# it, and **the first commit would have shipped a guard that fails on a fresh
# clone**: the guard checks the file exists and fails when it does not, which is
# correct and would have been the cloner's first impression of this project.
#
# The same shape as B-156, and found the same way — by asking git rather than by
# reading this file. The negation is scoped to the fixture directory rather than
# widened, and `gitignore_test.js` holds both directions.
!dev/tests/fixtures/**
dev/tests/fixtures/**/*.b105-bak

# ── Logs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ignored as a DIRECTORY rather than by extension: session logs record absolute
# paths, so they carry a home directory (328 occurrences across 28 files as of
# v3.14.153), and a non-.log file dropped in here would otherwise be committed.
logs/*
!logs/.gitkeep

# ── Packaging output ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Build artefacts belong in dev/archive/packaging/, not at the repository root.
# An abandoned zip temp sat at the root for two days before anyone noticed it.
*.zip

# ── Delivered-file landing folder ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The desktop app writes files it hands back into "Claude outputs/", and when the
# session's working folder IS this repository they land inside it. Measured at
# v3.14.400: two files, and BOTH were copies of tracked ones — `README.md`
# byte-identical to the live README, and `README-1.md` a renamed copy of
# `samples/README.md` that collided with the first.
#
# So the risk is not disk, it is a SECOND COPY OF A TRACKED FILE inside the
# working tree: someone edits the copy, and the edit is invisible to every guard
# that reads the real path. That is the shape `dev/*.html` is ignored for.
#
# The name carries a space, which is fine mid-pattern — only a TRAILING space
# needs escaping. Verified with `git check-ignore -v`, not by reading, per the
# note at the top of this file.
Claude outputs/

# ── OS noise ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
